{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790451695,"nonce":"xoKi5NT6FvmI7fKG8HDJ7fwfMMM=","title":"Post-opt HIR instruction order depends on ids consumed before the program is lowered","message":"The Python frontend reads a program a second time when a record shape is demoted (ad42115). For json_bench the second reading's typed AST is the same as a single reading's, yet the post-opt HIR differs: in library functions such as zb_set_index_entries the hoisted loads come out in a different order (the same instructions, renumbered). The base binary's dump is byte-identical run to run, so the order follows something the first reading advanced: TypeId::next(), interned string ids, or another global counter, iterated through a hash map in an optimisation pass (LICM's hoist order is the likely one).\n\nOn the NUC json_bench's per-iteration time moved by about 1.6% between the two binaries with no other code change in that program, so pass order that depends on id values is also a source of benchmark noise between commits. Reproduce: ZYNTAX_DUMP_HIR_DIR on a staged json_bench (kernel + shims/util.py + shims/optparse.py) with zypy from 3378b3a1 and from 622280c9, diff 001-post-opt-tiered-compile_module.hir.","files":null}]}